<%# The login cookie, disclosed at the point where a stranger might acquire one.

    WHY THIS IS CONDITIONAL ON SIGNUP BEING OPEN.

    Two different situations wear the same word "cookie" here:

    On the hosted service anyone can create an account, so the people who end up
    with our session cookie are members of the public who arrived from a search
    result. They are owed a plain statement of what got set and why, at the moment
    it becomes relevant, rather than a sentence in a policy they had no reason to
    open.

    On a self-hosted install the only accounts are the operator's own team, on
    infrastructure the operator controls, under a policy the operator writes. There
    is no public to disclose to, and a notice on an internal tool is furniture
    nobody reads. So it does not render there. `Tastatur.allow_signup?` is exactly
    the right switch: it is already false by default when self-hosted, and it
    tracks the actual question — can someone who is not you obtain an account here.

    WHY THIS IS NOT A CONSENT BANNER, and must never become one.

    A session cookie that exists solely to keep a signed-in user signed in is
    strictly necessary and exempt from consent under ePrivacy. There is nothing to
    accept and nothing to decline, so there are no buttons: an accept/reject dialog
    would be asking permission we do not need, and it would teach visitors that
    this product is the kind of thing that requires one. It is precisely the thing
    Tastatur exists to let people stop doing.

    So: a statement, not a gate. It blocks nothing, it does not overlay the page,
    and it never appears on a measured site — only on our own. -%>
<% if Tastatur.allow_signup? %>
  <aside class="rule pt-5 mt-8 text-[12px] text-muted leading-relaxed" aria-label="Cookie notice">
    <p>
      <strong class="text-ink-2">Cookies on this site.</strong>
      Signing in sets a first-party session cookie so you stay signed in, and a
      second one if you ask to be remembered. That is all of them: no advertising,
      no third parties, nothing shared. They are strictly necessary for a login, so
      there is nothing here to accept or decline.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-1.5">
      <%# The distinction worth drawing even here: this is about the reader's own
          account, not about anyone they go on to measure. Someone landing on a
          cookie notice published by an analytics company will reasonably assume
          the worst, and the answer is one click away. -%>
      Tastatur sets nothing at all on the visitors to sites you measure — that is
      the point of it.
      <%= link_to "How the two differ", privacy_path(anchor: "cookies"),
                  class: "underline hover:text-signal" %>.
    </p>
  </aside>
<% end %>
